From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rui Figueira <rfigueira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:16:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Let a cell's width size its column, not hold the table wide
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

In the auto table layout algorithm, a cell's length width raises the
max-content width of its column but not its min-content width
(css-tables-3 §3.9.4). Tables are laid out as grids, and a cell measured
its width as both, so a table could not shrink below the sum of its cell
widths. A Wikipedia table with header widths of 5em, 10em and 48em
stayed 1100px wide in a 772px column and ran under the figures floated
beside it.

A single-column cell with a length width now measures its min-content
width from its content alone. Its max-content width is still its width,
within its min-width and max-width, so the column also grows to the
max-content width of its other cells.

The content measurement uses the ContentSize sizing mode. Taffy keeps
one sizing mode per layout cache and drops the entries when the mode
changes. So the cell's cache keeps the result as an entry in the mode
that the grid asked in, under a parent width that no layout passes
(NaN). The entry lives as long as the cell's other sizes, and the mode
of the cache does not change. Inline layout ignores min-width and
max-width in ContentSize, so the table applies them to the result. It
also keeps the result at least as wide as the cell's padding and
border.

The spare width of a table goes to the columns without a cell width
while there are any. A column with a cell width gets a
minmax(min-content, max-content) track, which does not stretch. Its
minimum is min-content, not auto: an auto minimum takes a cell's
min-width, or zero for a scroll container, in place of the width of
the content. When no column is left to stretch, these columns get an
auto maximum and share the spare width.
